{"assets":[],"contentKind":"statement","contestId":"297","diagnostics":[],"document":{"children":[{"attrs":{"level":1,"role":"title"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"B. Fish Weight"}],"kind":"heading"},{"attrs":{"role":"time_limit"},"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"time limit per test"},{"kind":"text","text":":"}],"kind":"container"},{"kind":"text","text":"1 second"}],"kind":"container"},{"attrs":{"role":"memory_limit"},"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"memory limit per test"},{"kind":"text","text":":"}],"kind":"container"},{"kind":"text","text":"256 megabytes"}],"kind":"container"},{"attrs":{"role":"body"},"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"It is known that there are "},{"kind":"math_inline","text":"k"},{"kind":"text","text":" fish species in the polar ocean, numbered from "},{"kind":"math_inline","text":"1"},{"kind":"text","text":" to "},{"kind":"math_inline","text":"k"},{"kind":"text","text":". They are sorted by non-decreasing order of their weight, which is a positive number. Let the weight of the "},{"kind":"math_inline","text":"i"},{"kind":"text","text":"-th type of fish be "},{"kind":"math_inline","text":"wi"},{"kind":"text","text":", then "},{"kind":"math_inline","text":"0 < w1 ≤ w2 ≤ ... ≤ wk"},{"kind":"text","text":" holds."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Polar bears Alice and Bob each have caught some fish, and they are guessing who has the larger sum of weight of the fish he/she's caught. Given the type of the fish they've caught, determine whether it is possible that the fish caught by Alice has a "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"strictly larger"}],"kind":"container"},{"kind":"text","text":" total weight than Bob's. In other words, does there exist a sequence of weights "},{"kind":"math_inline","text":"wi"},{"kind":"text","text":" (not necessary integers), such that the fish caught by Alice has a strictly larger total weight?"}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"section"},{"attrs":{"role":"input_specification"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":2},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Input"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"The first line contains three integers "},{"kind":"math_inline","text":"n, m, k"},{"kind":"math_inline","text":"(1 ≤ n, m ≤ 105, 1 ≤ k ≤ 109)"},{"kind":"text","text":" — the number of fish caught by Alice and Bob respectively, and the number of fish species."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"The second line contains "},{"kind":"math_inline","text":"n"},{"kind":"text","text":" integers each from 1 to "},{"kind":"math_inline","text":"k"},{"kind":"text","text":", the list of fish type caught by Alice. The third line contains "},{"kind":"math_inline","text":"m"},{"kind":"text","text":" integers each from 1 to "},{"kind":"math_inline","text":"k"},{"kind":"text","text":", the list of fish type caught by Bob."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Note that one may have caught more than one fish for a same species."}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"section"},{"attrs":{"role":"output_specification"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":2},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Output"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Output \""},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"YES"}],"kind":"container"},{"kind":"text","text":"\" (without quotes) if it is possible, and \""},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"NO"}],"kind":"container"},{"kind":"text","text":"\" (without quotes) otherwise."}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"section"},{"attrs":{"role":"samples"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":2},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Examples"}],"kind":"heading"},{"attrs":{"role":"sample"},"children":[{"attrs":{"role":"sample_input"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Input"}],"kind":"heading"},{"kind":"code_block","text":"3 3 3\n2 2 2\n1 1 3\n"}],"kind":"section"},{"attrs":{"role":"sample_output"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Output"}],"kind":"heading"},{"kind":"code_block","text":"YES\n"}],"kind":"section"}],"kind":"section"},{"attrs":{"role":"sample"},"children":[{"attrs":{"role":"sample_input"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Input"}],"kind":"heading"},{"kind":"code_block","text":"4 7 9\n5 2 7 3\n3 5 2 7 3 8 7\n"}],"kind":"section"},{"attrs":{"role":"sample_output"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Output"}],"kind":"heading"},{"kind":"code_block","text":"NO\n"}],"kind":"section"}],"kind":"section"}],"kind":"section"},{"attrs":{"role":"note"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":2},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Note"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"In the first sample, if "},{"kind":"math_inline","text":"w1 = 1, w2 = 2, w3 = 2.5"},{"kind":"text","text":", then Alice has a total of "},{"kind":"math_inline","text":"2 + 2 + 2 = 6"},{"kind":"text","text":" weight units, while Bob only has "},{"kind":"math_inline","text":"1 + 1 + 2.5 = 4.5"},{"kind":"text","text":"."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"In the second sample, the fish that Alice caught is a subset of Bob's. Therefore, the total weight of Bob’s fish is always not less than the total weight of Alice’s fish."}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"section"}],"kind":"document"},"index":"B","problemCode":"297B","schema":2,"sourceKind":"html","sourceUrl":"https://codeforces.com/contest/297/problem/B"}